SIGNAL = r"""{\sffamily\bfseries\small\color{Deep}Per unit mass, brake- and tyre-wear PM$_{2.5}$ was 5--11 times more oxidatively and cytotoxically potent than exhaust PM$_{2.5}$ in dynamometer and in-vitro tests; weighted by that potency, non-exhaust sources already make up about eight times exhaust in China's 2023 on-road emissions and are projected to reach 96\,\% of the toxicity-adjusted total by 2050.}\\[1.4mm]
\footnotesize
Fudan University, \textit{Environmental Science \& Technology}. Potencies from dynamometer-generated brake, tyre and exhaust particles with chemical analysis and cell assays, applied to compiled national inventories; the non-exhaust share of toxicity-adjusted on-road emissions rises from 54\,\% (2014) to 96\,\% (2050) under electrification without dedicated controls; hotspots track dense traffic in developed regions.
\textbf{Where it is weak:} the 5--11-fold ratio rests on a small set of pads, tyres and engines and on acellular/in-vitro endpoints, and is then scaled nationally with no uncertainty reported; toxicity-weighted emissions are not exposure or health outcomes.
\textbf{Why it matters here:} a mass-calibrated optical sensor reads a microgram of tyre wear the same as a microgram of exhaust. If this ratio holds even roughly, roadside networks built on mass alone will understate the hazard trend precisely as fleets electrify --- the case for pairing PM$_{2.5}$ with an absorption, metals or oxidative-potential proxy near roads."""

